# A name asked of SLIRP's DNS forwarder (N3; `make test-net-fetch`, not on CI
# unless NET_DNS=1: it depends on the host's own resolver, which CI does not
# promise; the resolver's logic is covered by `cargo test -p zoraya-net` with
# a scripted DNS server). The guest is on QEMU's user network without
# `restrict=on`.
! panicked
! first process ended
< Type help to begin, or help tour.
< \r\n~>
> sleep 3\r
< \r\n~>
# A name asked of SLIRP's DNS forwarder at 10.0.2.3, which answers `localhost`
# from the host's own table (no Internet needed): the answer is 127.0.0.1, an
# address of this side of the network, so the connection is refused as local
# (ADR-0223 item 4). A name that does not exist (`.invalid`, RFC 2606) is
# unresolvable.
> fetch http://localhost:80/\r
< the name points at this machine's own network
< \r\n~>
> fetch http://nothing.invalid/\r
< the name has no address
< \r\n~>
# `ping` resolves a name inside the network service, under an echo-only grant for
# that name, with the same refusals as `fetch`.
> ping localhost\r
< the name points at this machine's own network
< \r\n~>
> ping nothing.invalid\r
< the name has no address
< \r\n~>
x power off
> poweroff\r
